Make memalign's blocks big enough to free.

A freed block may become a free-area of its own, whose descriptor is
written in its last bytes, so malloc and realloc never make a block
smaller than a descriptor.  memalign could: memalign(8, 1) on a 32-bit
machine made a block of 8 bytes, the 4-byte header and 4 more, and freeing
it wrote the 12-byte descriptor over the last 4 bytes of the block before.

--- a/libc/stdlib/malloc/memalign.c
+++ b/libc/stdlib/malloc/memalign.c
@@ -47,6 +47,12 @@
   /* Make SIZE something we like.  */
   size = HEAP_ADJUST_SIZE (size);
 
+  /* Because we sometimes must use a freed block to hold a free-area node,
+     we must make sure that every allocated block can hold one, as malloc
+     and realloc do.  */
+  if (size + MALLOC_HEADER_SIZE < sizeof (struct heap_free_area))
+    size = HEAP_ADJUST_SIZE (sizeof (struct heap_free_area) - MALLOC_HEADER_SIZE);
+
   /* Use malloc to do the initial allocation, since it deals with getting
      system memory.  We over-allocate enough to be sure that we'll get
      enough memory to hold a properly aligned block of size SIZE,
